From f8e3c3633d37031c55a112e84299b6cf09b4fc9b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 17:07:16 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] kernel: backport upstream Brahma-B53 mitigation whitelists

The HND 5.04 kernels run on Broadcom Brahma-B53 cores (BCM4912/BCM6756/
BCM4916, MIDR 0x420f1000), which are missing from every arm64 CPU
whitelist in the 4.19 kernel.  The B53 is an in-order core without the
speculation that Meltdown/Spectre-v2/SSB exploit; upstream declared it
immune, but only in v5.4/v5.6, and none of it was ever backported to
4.19.y (verified against 4.19.325, the EOL release).

As a result every HND 5.04 router enables KPTI and pays its cost on
every kernel entry/exit with no security benefit:

  meltdown: Mitigation: PTI  ->  Not affected

  getppid ns/call (aarch64 static, N=40 processes, pinned core):
    GT-AXE16000 (stock 3006.102.8_2 kernel vs same kernel + this patch)
        226.0 -> 177.8   (-21.3%)
    GT-BE98 (4916)
        224.4 -> 188.6   (-16.0%)

Verbatim transplant of three mainline commits, authored by Broadcom:
  bfc97f9f199c ("arm64: apply ARM64_ERRATUM_845719 workaround for
                Brahma-B53 core" - MIDR definition only)
  e059770cb1cd ("arm64: Brahma-B53 is SSB and spectre v2 safe")
  31d868c49017 ("arm64: kpti: Add Broadcom Brahma-B53 core to the
                KPTI whitelist")

Applied identically to both 5.04 SDKs (axhnd.675x / 4.19.183 and
behnd.4916 / 4.19.294).  Spectre-v1 mitigation (__user pointer
sanitization) is unaffected; nothing upstream considers required for
this core is disabled.

